            "value": "<div data-page-id=\"N0Dfd70vKo3C16x22MllfNEwg8e\" data-lark-html-role=\"root\" data-docx-has-block-data=\"false\">\n<div class=\"ace-line ace-line old-record-id-JAGld89cQotqu9xiHlflHIDwgCe\">As for the other destinations, Spain ranked third, welcoming 861,000 immigrants from the listed nations, four times higher than Italy's 196,000. Spain was the preferred country for British immigrants, with 391,000, nearly twice the numbers of Germans and Italians (around 200,000 each), while only 86,000 came from Poland. In Italy, Poles were the predominant group (106,000), far outnumbering the other three nationalities, whose figures ranged between 19,000 and 42,000. Finally, the total number of expatriates residing in Poland was only 6,000, among which, more than two-thirds were from Germany, followed by modest British and Italian communities of around 700 people each.</div>\n</div>",
